      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1644f617-777c-5135-8fad-25d26304c536",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Princess Mafalda of Savoy, Umberto II of Italy, Victor Emmanuel III, Elena of Montenegro\n- Products: SS Principessa Mafalda\n- Companies: NGI\n- Places: Italy, Genoa, Buenos Aires\n\nFacts:\n- Princess Mafalda of Savoy was born on 2 November 1902 and was the second daughter of King Victor Emmanuel III of Italy and his wife Elena of Montenegro.\n- In 1909, the ship SS Principessa Mafalda, named after Princess Mafalda of Savoy, entered NGI's South American service between Genoa and Buenos Aires.\n- Umberto II of Italy, born on 15 September 1904, was the younger brother of Princess Mafalda of Savoy.\n- From 1944, Umberto II of Italy was the de facto head of state of Italy.\n- Umberto II of Italy reigned as the last King of Italy for 34 days, from 9 May 1946 to 12 June 1946, and was nicknamed the May King.\n- Princess Mafalda of Savoy died on 27 August 1944.\n- Umberto II of Italy died on 18 March 1983.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7e0f0cf5-a420-554a-8ecc-633c36638c95",
        "text": "Page: Princess Mafalda of Savoy. Sentence: Princess Mafalda of Savoy (2 November 1902 – 27 August 1944) was the second daughter of King Victor Emmanuel III of Italy and his wife Elena of Montenegro.\nPage: Princess Mafalda of Savoy. Sentence: The future King Umberto II of Italy was her younger brother.\nPage: Umberto II of Italy. Sentence: Umberto II (Italian: \"Umberto Nicola Tommaso Giovanni Maria di Savoia\" ; 15 September 190418 March 1983), was the last King of Italy.\nPage: Umberto II of Italy. Sentence: He reigned for 34 days, from 9 May 1946 to 12 June 1946, although he had been \"de facto\" head of state since 1944, and was nicknamed the May King (Italian: Re di Maggio ).\nPage: SS Principessa Mafalda. Sentence: Named after Princess Mafalda of Savoy, second daughter of King Victor Emmanuel III, the ship entered NGI's South American service between Genoa and Buenos Aires in 1909.",
